  "script": "Players who went from having regular jobs to going pro! Shaka Hislop graduated from Howard University with honors in mechanical engineering, and while studying there, he landed a summer internship at NASA headquarters in Washington, D.C., where he worked with the scientific team on the Space Station Freedom project. After graduating, he got drafted by an indoor football team called the Baltimore Blast, but that's when someone in the crowd changed his life forever. In May of 1992, the Blast played a friendly match against Aston Villa, and a Reading FC scout who was there to watch Villa couldn't take his eyes off the 6'4\" goalkeeper on the other side of the pitch. Two months later, Reading offered Hislop a professional contract, and by September, he was in England playing in the Second Division, where he quickly became a fan favorite, winning the club's Player of the Year award twice and eventually earning a move to Newcastle United in the Premier League. But his career didn't peak there. Hislop went on to play for West Ham and Portsmouth, racking up over 220 Premier League appearances across more than a decade in England's top flight. Then, in 2006, at 37 years old, he represented Trinidad & Tobago at their first-ever World Cup, stepping onto the pitch against Sweden after an injury to their starting goalkeeper and helping his country hold the European quarterfinalists to a nil-nil draw.",
